::MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.7
Question:
A two-way table compares grade level and club participation. What are the two variables?
{
=A. grade level and club participation#Correct. These are the two categorical variables organized in the table.
~B. mean and median#Those are measures of center, not table variables.
~C. slope and intercept#Those describe a linear model, not a two-way table.
~D. sample and population#Those describe study design, not the table variables.
}
::MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.8
Question:
A report conclusion is stronger than the data support. What should you do?
{
~A. accept it because it is in a report#Reports still need to be evaluated.
=B. write a more cautious conclusion#Correct. A responsible interpretation matches the strength of the evidence.
~C. ignore all data in the report#Data can be useful when interpreted responsibly.
~D. change the sample size#A reader cannot change the original sample size.
}
::MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.9
Question:
A report surveys only volunteers. What is a possible limitation?
{
~A. perfect representation#Volunteers do not automatically represent the population.
~B. causation proof#Sampling does not prove cause and effect.
=C. voluntary response bias#Correct. Volunteers may differ from people who did not respond.
~D. no variables#A survey still has variables or responses.
}
::MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.7
Question:
What does relative frequency show in a two-way table?
{
~A. only the largest count#Relative frequency can be calculated for many categories.
~B. the exact cause of a result#A table does not prove cause.
~C. the title of the table#The title describes the table but is not a frequency.
=D. a part of a total, often as a percent#Correct. Relative frequency compares a count to a total.
}
::MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L07_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.8
Question:
Which is the best question to ask about a statistical report?
{
~A. Is the font easy to read?#Visual design alone does not validate the conclusion.
=B. Does the conclusion match the data and sample?#Correct. This checks evidence, sampling, and claim strength.
~C. Can I ignore the sample?#The sample affects the strength of the conclusion.
~D. Does it use the word proven?#The word proven may be a warning, not proof.
}
